    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d710">

                <pb facs="0758" n="744" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg744"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">103.9169/3943: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bowers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-08T14:00:00-04:00">June 8, 1944—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:17 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>995. For Foreign Economic Administration. Reference Department’s circular airgram
                    May 25, 11:15 a.m.<anchor n="64" xml:id="d710-f1"/> Country Agency has been
                    consulted but must present matter of further roll back to a minimum positive
                    list to Board of Directors for decision which is expected to be rendered about
                    June 13. In the meantime, Adriasola, manager of the Country Agency, has assured
                    Embassy that he believes the Country Agency will agree. As soon as possible
                    after the definite <pb facs="0759" n="745" xml:id="pg745"/> decision of the
                    Board, the Embassy will inform the Department by telegraph.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d710-f1" n="64" xml:id="d710-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it conveyed a proposal to require import recommendations only for
                    commodities on a positive list, effective July 1, 1944. The most important items
                    listed were steel, cotton, rubber, rayon, wood, and their products
                    (103.9169/3680a).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
